ntifies that graph with a single application of \(\mathbf{V}\). `Vcomb_isRetract` rewrites (5.19) as the retract equation \(\mathbf{V} = \mathrm{graph}(a \mapsto \mathbf{V}(\mathbf{V}(a)))\). `theorem_5_5_universe` is the theorem as stated: \(\mathbf{V}\) is a closure because it contains the identity combinator and is that retract, and a set is a closure if and only if it is typed by \(\mathbf{V}\), which is the equality \(a = \mathbf{V}(a)\) read in either direction. The monotonicity sandwich (5.20)–(5.21), the universal property that \(\mathbf{V}(a)\) is the least closure above \(a\), and the assertion that the lattice of closures is algebraic are not stated; the retract of \(\mathbf{P}\omega\) is `Vcomb_isRetract`. `eq_5_22`, `eq_5_23`, and `eq_5_24` conclude only that the boxed product, boxed sum, and function space of two elements typed by \(\mathbf{V}\) are again typed by \(\mathbf{V}\), by converting the typings to closure hypotheses, applying the product, sum, or function-space theorem, and converting back; they do not type the curried combinators as elements of \(\mathbf{V} \circ\to (\mathbf{V} \circ\to \mathbf{V})\). **Theorem:** partial — \(\mathbf{V}\) is a closure whose typed points are the closures, while (5.22)–(5.24) give only the pointwise closure of the three operations. **Proof:** partial — (5.16), (5.17), (5.18), and (5.19) are present, with the converse of (5.18) expanded into the graph argument above, and (5.20) and (5.21) are not.
